[NAME]
creature-controller \- drive an animatronic creature from DMX and stream its audio

[DESCRIPTION]
.B creature-controller
receives DMX frames for one creature (E1.31 over UDP, or RS\-485), turns them into
motor positions, and streams them over USB serial to the creature's Raspberry Pi
Pico board. It also plays the creature's voice and background music from the
Creature Server's Opus/RTP multicast streams, in time with the motion, and
advertises the creature over mDNS.
.PP
On a creature host it is not started by hand. A udev rule starts one instance
per plugged\-in board, as described under
.BR SERVICE .
The command\-line options below are for running it manually and for discovery.

[SERVICE]
The package installs the udev rule
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-creature\-controller.rules
and the template unit
.IR creature\-controller@.service .
When a board from April's Creature Workshop appears, the rule reads its USB
product name
.RI ( NAME ,
from the board's EEPROM), creates
.IR /dev/creature/NAME ,
and starts
.BR creature\-controller@NAME.service .
Unplugging the board stops the instance. There is nothing to enable.
.PP
The unit runs
.I /usr/lib/creature\-controller/start\-creature NAME
as the
.B creature\-controller
system user, which belongs to the
.B dialout
and
.B audio
groups. That script lower\-cases
.IR NAME ,
selects the creature and controller configuration files listed under
.BR FILES ,
and runs
.B creature\-controller
with
.BR \-\-config ,
.B \-\-creature\-config
and
.BR "\-\-serial\-device /dev/creature/NAME" .
.PP
If the link to the board fails, the controller exits non\-zero and systemd
restarts it after 30 seconds, up to 10 starts in 10 minutes. Package upgrades
never restart a running instance, so an upgrade cannot move a creature or clear
an emergency stop; restart it when the creature is safe.

[CONFIGURATION]
Two JSON files configure a running controller, both selected by the board's
name in lower case.
.TP
.B Creature configuration
.RB ( \-\-creature\-config )
describes the creature itself: its motors, inputs, channel offset, audio channel
and its required power limits
.RB ( power_draw_limit_watts ,
.BR power_draw_warning_watts ,
.BR power_draw_response_seconds ).
A creature without its power limits will not start. These files come only from
the package.
.TP
.B Controller configuration
.RB ( \-\-config )
describes this host: the Creature Server
.RB ( creatureServer ),
the network interface and E1.31 universe, RTP audio and the sound device
.RB ( useRTPAudio ,
.BR audioDeviceName ,
gains, limiter and ALSA mixer control), playout timing
.RB ( commonPlayoutDelayMs ,
.BR audioDeviceCompensationMs ),
the serial boards
.RB ( UARTs ,
unless
.B \-\-serial\-device
is given), temperature and load limits, and
.BR logLevel .
.PP
To change one host without rebuilding the package, copy its controller file from
.I /usr/share/creature\-controller/controllers/
to
.I /etc/creature/controller/
and edit it there. The file in
.I /etc
takes precedence, and upgrades leave it alone. The full description of every
field is in
.IR /usr/share/doc/creature\-controller/README.md .

[FILES]
.TP
.I /usr/share/creature\-controller/creatures/NAME.json
Creature configuration, one per creature, shipped by the package.
.TP
.I /usr/share/creature\-controller/controllers/NAME.json
Default controller configuration for the host that runs each creature.
.TP
.I /etc/creature/controller/NAME.json
Optional per\-host override of the controller configuration. Used instead of the
packaged file when present.
.TP
.I /dev/creature/NAME
Serial device for the creature's board, created by the udev rule.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/creature\-controller/start\-creature
Wrapper the service runs; selects the configuration files for a board name.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/creature\-controller@.service
Template unit, one instance per board. Override it with a drop\-in under
.IR /etc/systemd/system/creature\-controller@.service.d/ .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-creature\-controller.rules
Starts and stops instances as boards come and go.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/sysusers.d/creature\-controller.conf
Declares the
.B creature\-controller
system user.
.TP
.I /etc/avahi/services/creature\-controller\-ssh.service
Advertises SSH on the host over mDNS.
.TP
.I /var/run/ptp4lro
ptp4l's read\-only management socket, used to check PTP clock sync.

[EXAMPLES]
List the sound and network devices this host can use:
.PP
.RS
.nf
creature\-controller \-\-list\-sound\-devices
creature\-controller \-\-list\-network\-devices
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a running creature:
.PP
.RS
.nf
systemctl list\-units 'creature\-controller@*'
journalctl \-u creature\-controller@Caroll.service \-f
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start a creature again after its instance has failed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo systemctl reset\-failed creature\-controller@Caroll.service
sudo systemctl start creature\-controller@Caroll.service
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find every running creature on the network:
.PP
.RS
.nf
avahi\-browse \-rt _creature\-controller._udp
.fi
.RE

[SEE ALSO]
.BR creature\-audio\-router (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR udev (7)
